var text = '''[center][b]Transformation[/b][/center]
[i]Sameness Under Change[/i]

A transformation does not create a new object.
It changes the relation between an object and space.

The cube you see moving up and down,
the cube rotating in place,
and the cube changing size
are not different cubes.

They are the same cube,
acted upon by different transformations.

[hr]

[b]The Static Cube Is Not More True[/b]

It may seem that the still cube is the "original"
and the transformed cubes are variations.

This is a mistake.

A cube at rest is not more fundamental
than a cube in motion.
A cube aligned to the grid is not more correct
than a cube rotated away from it.
A cube at scale one is not more real
than a cube scaled up or down.

The difference is not in the object.
The difference is in the operation applied to it.

[hr]

[b]Three Fundamental Transformations[/b]

In computational space, all visible change
can be reduced to three operations:

• Translation — changing position  
• Rotation — changing orientation  
• Scale — changing size  

These operations do not alter what the cube is.
They alter how the cube relates to space,
to the grid, and to your body.

[hr]

[b]Composition[/b]

Transformations can be combined.

A cube can move while rotating.
It can rotate while scaling.
It can scale while moving.

The spinning pickup cube demonstrates this:
multiple transformations acting together,
without destroying the cube’s identity.

Order matters.
Changing the order changes the result.

[hr]

[b]Why This Matters[/b]

Transformation is the moment geometry stops being static.

Up to now, forms were defined.
From here on, forms are acted upon.

Movement, interaction, physics, and systems
all depend on transformation.

This is not decoration.
This is the beginning of change.

[hr]

[color=cyan][b]Observe:[/b][/color]
Every cube here is equivalent.
None is privileged.
None is more correct.

Only the transformations differ.'''
